australia is known to be  responsible for a chunk of emissions  then we can say that they have changed  the climate and thus are responsible for  the damage caused if only it were that  simple unfortunately though i'm not a  lawyer so i don't have the knowledge to  explain to you quite why it's not that  simple  but i do just so happen to have  youtube's lawyer on speed dial i regret  to inform you that to date environmental  lawsuits have had very little success in  preventing climate change or redressing  the changes to the planet that have  already occurred and there are some very  good reasons for that in the u.s legal  system and really most of the legal  systems that follow in the english  tradition there are a number of  requirements that you have to meet  before you're able to file a lawsuit and  these types of environmental lawsuits  are incredibly difficult to actually  prosecute or move forward  first  generally a plaintiff to be able to sue  someone has to show injury in fact and  the injury in fact requirement requires  that  what the harm that you have suffered is  concrete and particularized  and it's not conjectural or hypothetical  so in other words even if  there are going to be real concrete  harms in the future you have to show  that you have suffered them in the  present or in the past on top of that  the injury that the plaintiff has  suffered needs to be actually  redressable by the court in other words  the court needs to be able to solve your  problem and  climate change that affects the entire  world  is not necessarily the type of thing  that any single court can solve or  provide damages for and then perhaps the  most important requirement generally to  being able to sue someone or something  is that the plaintiff has to show a  causal connection between the injury and  the conduct complained of in other words  it has to be fairly traceable to the  defendant's particular actions and when  it comes to climate change or the  environment this is particularly  difficult imagine you have a homeowner  who suffers damages related to a fire if  you can prove that someone started the  fire by lighting a match and setting  everything on fire that's probably not  hard to prove  in terms of the causal connection  now imagine you have two people both  with matches who set a fire at exactly  the same time that fire combines into  one fire and then it burns down  someone's house well now that's a little  bit harder but imagine still that you  have a thousand different people all  with matches setting a fire at the same  time  showing that your damages are related to  any one particular person it might not  be impossible but now it's getting more  dangerous then on top of it imagine you  have a situation where  i set up a rube goldberg machine that at  the end of it lights a match and starts  the fire now i don't actually start the  marble that sets the whole thing in  motion but somebody else does now who's  at fault is it the person that built the  rube goldberg machine or is it the  person that actually set it in motion  every time you get to a level of  abstraction it gets harder and harder to  be able to show a causal connection now  imagine in the climate change context  when you're dealing with say carbon  dioxide a metric ton of carbon dioxide  on its own probably has zero effect but  when you add thousands if not millions  of polluters all adding carbon dioxide  or methane well together that might  create some of the things that we're  talking about but maybe not alone so how  do you get damages from one particular  person who might be polluting when  there's thousands and thousands of other  people who have to contribute in order  for that to make a difference it's a  really really thorny question when it  comes to suing someone and trying to  stop it the problem with many lawsuits  to date is that they've focused on  emissions to date and thus the impact  that corporations and governments have  already had on the climate but here's  the thing  emissions are still ongoing and  unfortunately are going to continue into  the near future at the very least but  that means there's a whole different  possible category of lawsuit instead of  focusing on the past  focusing on holding corporations and  governments responsible for future  changes to the climb a landmark ruling  took place in 2015 in the netherlands  when a court ordered the dutch  government to cut its greenhouse gas  emissions as a failure to do so would  contribute to a climate that would pose  a quote serious degree of danger to  dutch citizens a ruling that was later  upheld by the dutch supreme court to  quote joanna setzer an expert in climate  litigation at the grantham institute for  the first time a court had recognized  that a government violates its duty of  care for citizens if it doesn't do  enough to curb emissions this meant a  lot from a legal point of view and since  that case strengthened by the precedent  and by the paris agreement and the  ipcc's fifth and now sixth assessment  reports more and more courts are ruling  in favor of climate-based plaintiffs and  as we strengthen our understanding of  both the causes of and effects of  climate change the type of litigation  brought to court is broadening  corporations are increasingly finding  themselves targeted by lawsuits that  aren't interested in gross carbon  emissions but things like green washing  corporations overselling the actions  they're taking to limit their  environmental impact or just outright  deception governments are also being  targeted in different more precise ways  such as seeking to remove specific  subsidies for the fossil fuel industry  or challenging permits being granted for  fossil fuel extraction or for the  expansion of airports though yeah that's  a pretty sore point for me in the  bristol area right now we can be pretty  confident that these strategic lawsuits  and their mirror image those filed by  individuals or corporations seeking to  prevent climate-based action will only  get more common in the next few years  honestly in terms of maximizing the  probability of success for using the  legal system  your best bet is probably changing the  law the things that i've talked about  are bedrock principles of litigation and  i don't think those things are going to  change anytime soon but  you can always write a law that allows  people to file suit that changes some of  those requirements the things that i've  been talking about are sort of the  default positions of the legal system  they exist well there isn't a contrary  law but that's the thing laws can be  changed and when you pass a law and  enact a statute well that changes the  default position of the legal system and  it would probably be pretty easy for  various governments to institute laws  that would allow someone to have  standing to file these kinds of lawsuits  governments could also give the power to  regulatory agencies to file suit on  behalf of the government so there are  certainly lots of ways for the default  position of the legal system to change  but at the moment given the default  rules it would be very very difficult so  you know  call your congressperson this is of  course a theme in climate change  generally speaking entire systems not  just the details in them need to change  from how we heat our homes to how  international trade is done but  especially with the dutch 2015 case  providing a legal precedent i am and  some environmental lawyers are growing  in confidence that more legal successes  lie in the near future climate  litigation will increasingly form part  of the silver buckshot used to try and  prevent catastrophic climate change this  century and if the past few years are  anything to go by it's going to be an  effective tool and yes winning a lawsuit  doesn't mean that emissions will fall  you have to enforce the ruling and yes  the whole system could frankly do with  an overhaul bu